---
status: approved
intent: >
  Observer pass: make the one-per-machine observer mandatory and the single
  source of truth for a project's sessions, with three MCP tools (start with
  the port list and the one-live-session HALT, event, end-session), hook
  presence and liveness, the event log, board reader, status builder with
  named pipelines and session-scoped rails, and the composing HTTP layer.
---

## Commitments

1. **Two MCP tools, one job each.** (Amended 2026-11-23, one observer per machine.) `aide_observer_init` is removed. `aide_observer_start` takes no port (no input) and follows commitments 33 and 34: it starts the observer on the fixed port, keeps a running same-or-newer observer, or replaces a running older one; in every success case its reply is the link to the calling session's view (commitment 26). When the port is held by another program it does not start and its reply names the port; it never picks another port. `aide_observer_event` records one event (commitment 5) and returns the post outcome. (Amended 2026-11-25, observer pass, mandatory observer: three MCP tools now. `aide_observer_start` no longer stops at a busy port: it walks the port list of commitment 73, and after the observer is up it runs the one-live-session check of commitment 69 before returning the link. No port on the list works → the blocker reply of commitment 70. `aide_observer_event` replies as commitment 5 says, now naming the session id. New third tool `aide_observer_end_session` (commitment 74).)

2. **Instructions-file lines are the agent's, never a tool's.** (Amended 2026-11-23, one observer per machine.) There is no `Observer port:` line. `Observer: off` in the project's instructions file (CLAUDE.md) means the agent skips `aide_observer_start` at boot (events are still recorded). No tool reads or writes the instructions file. (Amended 2026-11-25, observer pass, mandatory observer: superseded. The observer is mandatory: there is no `Observer: off` line, no project setting and no per-project way to skip start or recording. No tool reads or writes the instructions file. A host project still carrying an `Observer: off` line gets a `[migration]` (Detect: the line is present; Migrate: the aide-upgrader deletes that one line with the user's OK and tells the user the observer is always on; Confirm: per file). A per-session "do not observe this" from the user is skill-only (the aide-observer skill makes no observer calls for the rest of that session); nothing is stored and no tool knows of it.)

3. **Boot call order in the /aide skill.** (Amended 2026-11-23, one observer per machine.) (a) `aide_observer_event` with kind `session_started` carrying the agent-reported project root, kanban tag and brain folder (each omitted when the agent does not know it). (b) `aide_observer_start()` unless `Observer: off`. Then each action is reported via `aide_observer_event` (kinds in commitment 6). A failed or skipped call never stops, retries in a loop, or changes the pipeline. (Amended 2026-11-25, observer pass, mandatory observer: superseded by this order, owned by the new `aide-observer` skill (prose, edited by the main agent), which `/aide` invokes before the `aide` skill; the `aide` skill keeps no observer text. (a) `aide_observer_event` kind `session_started` (projectRoot?, kanbanTag?, brainPath? as before); its reply names the observer `sessionId` (commitment 71). (b) `aide_observer_event` kind `prompt`. (c) `aide_observer_start()`, always. Its reply is the link, HALT (commitment 69) or the no-port blocker (commitment 70). HALT and the blocker are hard stops: the agent tells the user and proceeds only after the user approves `aide_observer_end_session` on the other session (then start again) or closes this one. The agent gives the user the link once boot completes. Then every action is reported (kinds in commitments 6 and 68). A failed or skipped report never stops the pipeline except through commitment 21.)

4. **Event record shape.** One JSON object per line: `v` (schema version, starts at `1`), `ts` (ISO-8601 UTC, stamped by the server), `sessionId` (stamped by the server), `seq` (stamped by the server), `kind`, plus the per-kind fields of commitment 6. The agent supplies only `kind` and the per-kind fields. One shared event schema is used by the recorder (to validate before append), the reader (to skip malformed lines) and the observer endpoint (to reject bad posts).

5. **Record first, then notify.** `event/record` stamps the event, ALWAYS appends one line to the session's log file (commitment 7), and only then, if `observer.json` names a running observer, POSTs the event with the token, capped at ~250 ms. Every post failure (no file, stale pid, refused, timeout, non-2xx) is swallowed. The tool reply is `recorded` when the post landed, and `recorded; observer not running` when it did not. A failed append is also swallowed (the tool still returns, never errors) and reported in the reply. (Amended 2026-11-25, observer pass, mandatory observer: before appending, record checks for an ended mark on the calling session (commitment 72); when present it appends nothing, does not post, and replies `refused: session ended` (exact string the architect's). Every reply names the calling `sessionId`. The `recorded` / `recorded; observer not running` replies and the swallow-everything rule are unchanged.)

6. **Event kinds and per-kind fields.** `session_started` (projectRoot?, kanbanTag?, brainPath?); `prompt` (text, the full prompt); `task` (slug, title?, action `picked` | `moved`, column?); `phase_started` (phase, agent, module); `phase_finished` (phase, agent, module, result?, summary?); `gate_waiting` (gate, file: path of the file awaiting approval); `gate_resolved` (gate, file, resolution?); `plan_step_checked` (module, step, total?); `qa_verdict` (module, verdict `passed` | `issues`, open?); `file_written` (path, action `created` | `updated`); `commit` (sha, message?); `release` (version); `error` (message, agent?, module?). Field names, bounds and enums are final per commitment 16. (Amended 2026-11-25, observer pass, mandatory observer: schema v2 adds `pipeline_started`, `pipeline_finished` and `phase_skipped` and a `pipeline` field on existing kinds; see commitment 68. The kinds and fields above are otherwise unchanged.)

7. **Per-user app-data location, never the project.** (Amended 2026-11-23, one observer per machine.) App-data root per OS: Windows `%LOCALAPPDATA%\aidemd\`; macOS `~/Library/Application Support/aidemd/`; Linux `$XDG_STATE_HOME/aidemd/`, falling back to `~/.local/state/aidemd/`. Each project has its own folder `<root>/<project-key>/`, holding session logs at `<root>/<project-key>/events/<sessionId>.jsonl`. `observer.json` lives once per user at `<root>/observer.json`, not in any project folder. `<project-key>` is `<project-folder-name>-<short hash of the normalized absolute project root>`. Nothing is ever written under any project root. (Amended 2026-11-25, observer pass, mandatory observer: each project folder also holds hook presence and ended marks beside, never inside, the session logs (commitment 72).) (Amended 2026-11-25, packaged-app AppData fix, user: the Windows root is now `%USERPROFILE%\.aidemd\`, not `%LOCALAPPDATA%\aidemd\`, and `AIDEMD_HOME` overrides the root on every OS; see commitments 105–107.)

8. **Session identity and ordering.** One `sessionId` per MCP server process, generated once at process start. `seq` starts at 1 and increases by 1 per recorded event within the session. The MCP server process is the only writer of its session's file. A second `session_started` within one process is not recorded (re-boot or observer restart mid-session must not open a new session). (Amended 2026-11-25, observer pass, mandatory observer: the session's MCP server process stays the only writer of its log. Hook presence (written by `aidemd-mcp hook` processes) and ended marks (written by the hook on SessionEnd or by `aide_observer_end_session`) live in their own files beside the log (commitment 72), so no second process ever appends to a log. The second-`session_started` rule is unchanged.)

9. **observer.json.** (Amended 2026-11-23, one observer per machine.) Written once per user at the app-data root (commitment 7) by the observer when it starts: `{ address, token, pid, version }`, where `address` is `http://127.0.0.1:<port>`, `token` is a fresh random secret per observer start, and `version` is the running observer's AIDE package version. Written only after the bind succeeds; removed on exit only if it still names this observer's pid and token. A file whose `pid` is not alive is treated as "not running". (Amended 2026-11-25, observer pass, mandatory observer: the record also carries `port`, the port the observer took from the list of commitment 73, and `address` is built from it. Tools, the view command, the hook ping and every link read the port from this record, never assume the first port of the list.)

10. **Observer intake.** (Amended 2026-11-23, one observer per machine.) On start the observer replays every session file in every project folder's `events/` under the app-data root once, then receives events only through its POST endpoint, which checks the `Host` header (loopback names only) and the token, validates against the shared schema, and dedupes on `(sessionId, seq)`. Posts arriving during replay are held and merged through the same dedupe. No file watching anywhere in the feature. Events are pushed live to the browser; each project's status is rebuilt on every event and every page load. (Amended 2026-11-25, observer pass, mandatory observer: the observer also takes presence pings (commitment 75): on a ping it re-reads that session's presence and ended mark from disk, recomputes liveness, rebuilds the project's status and pushes it. A ping carries no state of its own. Replay also reads every project's presence and ended marks.)

11. **Status inputs and rules.** `status/` is a pure builder over (project files, events, board) as given; it does no I/O of its own beyond what it is handed or reads from the project's `.aide` files. Files decide current state; events add who, when, history and which file to surface (commitment 25). Status derives no per-module next step and does not mirror the /aide Resume Protocol. A gate shows as awaiting the user only when a `gate_waiting` event names the file AND the file's own status still awaits approval (e.g. `plan.aide` `status: proposed`). Plan/todo checkbox progress counts equal `aide_cleanup`'s open-item count for the same file. (Amended 2026-11-25, observer pass, mandatory observer: status also takes, as inputs, each session's liveness (commitment 76) and the events of earlier sessions of the project (ended and archived included, deleted gone) for resuming pipelines (commitment 79). It re-judges no liveness and reads no presence, ended mark or session log itself.)

12. **Board location.** (Amended 2026-11-23, one observer per machine.) `kanban/` locates a project's board from that project's newest session's `session_started` (brainPath + kanbanTag). A `--brain` path passed to the view command overrides brainPath only for the project the command was run from; every other project uses its own reported brainPath. It never reads `.aide/config/brain.aide` or any instructions file. No path, missing path or missing tag folder → `unavailable` with reason and a hint to pass `--brain`. An unparseable task note → a task marked `unreadable`. Task reads are confined to that project's board folders (no slug path escape). Read-only.

13. **The only control.** (Amended 2026-11-23, one observer per machine.) Deleting a session removes only `<root>/<project-key>/events/<sessionId>.jsonl` of the project the session belongs to, and is refused for a live session. The shutdown request of commitment 34 is not a user control and is never offered on the page. (Amended 2026-11-25, observer pass, mandatory observer: two operations, both on ended sessions only. Ending (`aide_observer_end_session`, commitment 74) writes an ended mark and deletes nothing. Archiving and deleting stay the page's controls; delete removes only that ended session's log, presence and ended mark under its project folder, is refused for a session not ended, and never touches the project. Nothing ends a session automatically.)

14. **Composition.** The HTTP layer (`src/observer`) composes `event` (read/delete), `kanban` and `status`. `status`, `kanban` and `event` never import each other. (Amended 2026-11-25, observer pass, mandatory observer: presence, ended marks and the liveness judgement live in `event` (touchPresence, endSession, readPresence, readTranscriptActivity, judgeLiveness). `status` receives liveness and earlier-session events as inputs from `src/observer` and still never imports `event` or `kanban`. The hook CLI and the observer tools reach presence only through `event`.)

15. **The writer's pid is stamped on every event.** The server-stamped envelope of commitment 4 is `v`, `ts`, `sessionId`, `seq` and `pid` (the MCP server process's pid). The agent never supplies any of them; an input carrying one is rejected.

16. **Final event fields.** Commitment 6's field names and optional marks are final. Strings are non-empty and bounded (labels 200, paths 1024, summaries/messages 4000, prompt text 100 000 characters). `phase` is the closed enum `spec | research | synthesize | structure | plan | build | qa | fix | align | refactor | cleanup`; `gate` is a free bounded string (the file it names is what status checks); `plan_step_checked.step` is a string such as `3` or `3a`; `commit.sha` is 7–40 lowercase hex. Event objects are strict: unknown keys are rejected by the recorder, the reader and the observer endpoint alike. All of it lives in `src/service/observer/event/shared/schema` as named exports `inputSchema` (agent-supplied) and `schema` (stored and posted).

17. **Wire contract shared from `event`.** `src/service/observer/event/shared/protocol` (default export: events route `/api/events`, token header `x-aide-observer-token`) and `src/service/observer/event/shared/observerRecord` (named `schema` for `observer.json`: strict `{ address: "http://127.0.0.1:<port>", token, pid }`) are the single source for both the recorder and `src/observer`. (Amended in the fix round, QA 2026-11-22: `protocol` also carries `indexMarker: "aide-observer"`, the `observer` value `GET /` answers with and the single-instance probe checks, and `bindHost: "127.0.0.1"`, the only address the observer binds; `src/observer` declares neither itself.) (Amended 2026-11-23, one observer per machine.) The `observerRecord` schema gains `version` (commitment 9). `protocol` also carries the shutdown route (commitment 34) and the default port (commitment 33); route names and the `GET /` probe answer carrying the running version are the architect's to settle. (Amended 2026-11-25, observer pass, mandatory observer: `protocol` also carries the presence ping route (commitment 75) and the port list (commitment 73); `observerRecord` gains `port` (commitment 9).)

18. **Session liveness.** A session is live while the pid on its highest-`seq` event is a running process OR that event is under 30 minutes old; otherwise it has ended. A session with no valid events is not live. `event/read` returns a `live` flag per session and `event/delete` refuses a live session by the same rule, so the page and the control never disagree. (Amended 2026-11-25, observer pass, mandatory observer: superseded by commitment 76. No pid check and no 30-minute event-age rule. A session is live unless it is ended (commitment 76); `event/read` returns the commitment-76 liveness per session instead of a `live` flag, and delete refuses anything not ended.)

19. **Project key.** `<project-key>` in commitment 7 is computed from the recording server's own resolved project root (never the agent-reported one): folder name + `-` + the first 6 hex of SHA-256 of the normalized absolute root, lowercased first on Windows only. Owned by `src/util/runtimePaths`. (Amended 2026-11-23, one observer per machine.) The observer has no project key of its own; it enumerates project folders under the app-data root (commitment 35), and `runtimePaths` also resolves the root-level `observer.json` path.

20. **Session id format.** `YYYYMMDDTHHmmssSSSZ-<6 lowercase hex>` (UTC creation time + random suffix), generated once per MCP server process. Plain string order is chronological order, so `event/read` returns sessions oldest first.

21. **Restart-once bookkeeping.** After a `recorded; observer not running` reply the agent tries `aide_observer_start` once; the attempt is spent until a post lands again, then reset (one attempt per run of failed posts). A failed attempt is told to the user once per run. This is the `/aide` skill's bookkeeping; no tool tracks it. (Amended 2026-11-25, observer pass, mandatory observer: the restart is still tried once per run of failed posts, but a failed restart (no port on the list works, commitment 70) is now a blocker: the agent stops and tells the user; nothing proceeds unobserved. A single missed post never blocks by itself. Still the skill's bookkeeping (now the aide-observer skill); no tool tracks it.)

22. **Board columns on disk.** The board folder is `<brainPath>/kanban/<kanbanTag>/` with column folders `backlog`, `todo`, `in-progress`, `in-review`, `done`, shown as Backlog, Todo, In Progress, In Review, Done (the brain's kanban guide). `archive/` is not a column and is never read. A missing column folder is an empty column, not an unavailable board. Unavailable reasons are exactly `no-brain-path`, `no-kanban-tag`, `brain-path-missing`, `board-folder-missing`, plus `board-read-failed` (amended in the fix round, QA 2026-11-22), which kanban never returns itself: the observer sets it when a board read throws, with the error in `detail`. Session marks come from the slugs named in the `## Kanban` section of `.aide/session.aide`.

23. **Liveness shown in three states.** Status splits commitment 18 for display: `live` = highest-`seq` event under 30 minutes old; `idle` = older, but its pid is running; `ended` = neither. `ended` is exactly commitment 18's "not live", so a session the page shows as live or idle is one `event/delete` refuses. (Amended 2026-11-25, observer pass, mandatory observer: superseded by commitment 76: states are `working`, `waiting` and `ended`, with `quiet` (no activity for 15 minutes) shown as "quiet N min, maybe stuck" on a working or waiting session. The page shows exactly what status was given.)

24. **Types cross modules only through `src/types`.** `event`, `kanban` and `status` keep their types in their own `types/` (event: `shared/schema`) folders, and `src/types/index.ts` re-exports them type-only; `status` names `ObserverEvent` and `BoardResult` through `@/types`, never by importing `event` or `kanban` (keeps commitment 14). (Amended 2026-11-25, observer pass, mandatory observer: the new liveness, presence and pipeline types (commitments 68, 72, 76) are re-exported type-only through `src/types` the same way; `status` names them through `@/types`. `InitCategory` and `UpgradeCategory` gain `hooks`.)

25. **Full tree always visible; a waiting event surfaces its file.** (User decision 2026-11-22.) The status's intent tree lists every AIDE file in the project (`.aide` specs, `brief.aide`, `plan.aide`, `todo.aide`) with its own status and checkbox progress, always, and the user can open any of them. Status also names at most one file to surface: the file of an open `gate_waiting` event (no later `gate_resolved` for the same file) whose own status still awaits approval. The page opens that file exactly as if the user had clicked it in the tree. The surfaced file clears on `gate_resolved` for that file or when the file no longer awaits approval; a file that no longer exists surfaces nothing (no placeholder, no error card).

26. **Reads need the token too.** (Settled 2026-11-22.) Every request to the observer (page, API reads, the browser push stream, event posts and the shutdown request) requires the observer's token; the page link carries it as `?token=<token>`. (Amended 2026-11-23, one observer per machine.) `aide_observer_start` returns the full link to the calling session's view (the observer address, the session's view path for its project and session, and `?token=<token>`), for a newly started, kept or replacing observer alike. The view command prints a full tokened link too (target: commitment 40). (Amended 2026-11-24, user decision: the page shell and the allowlisted page assets are the only exception; see commitments 52 and 67. Every data route still requires the token.) (Amended 2026-11-25, observer pass, mandatory observer: the link uses the port in `observer.json` (commitment 9).)

27. **Phase names are a closed list.** (Settled 2026-11-22; confirms commitment 16.) `phase` is exactly one of `spec`, `research`, `synthesize`, `structure`, `plan`, `build`, `qa`, `fix`, `align`, `refactor`, `cleanup`; an event carrying any other phase is rejected by the recorder, the reader and the observer endpoint.

28. **One process-alive check in `src/util`.** (Settled 2026-11-22.) The check whether a pid is a running process lives in `src/util` and is shared by `event` (session liveness, commitment 18; stale `observer.json`, commitment 9) and `status` (three-state liveness, commitment 23). Neither module carries its own copy, so the page and the delete control cannot disagree (keeps commitment 14: shared via `src/util`, not via each other). (Amended 2026-11-25, observer pass, mandatory observer: no longer used for session liveness (commitment 76). It remains only for the stale-`observer.json` check of commitment 9 and the old-observer wait of commitment 47.)

29. **Kanban card fields.** (Settled 2026-11-22.) A board card shows the task's frontmatter `type` as its own chip, separate from its tags, and its tags come from the task's frontmatter `tags`.

30. **Which file is surfaced, and when the page opens it.** (User, 2026-11-22; settles the two commitment-25 open questions.) Status surfaces the file of the newest open `gate_waiting` event whose file still awaits approval: `plan.aide`, `todo.aide` or `brief.aide` with `status: proposed`, or an intent spec that is present (it clears only on its `gate_resolved`). Status lists every such file newest first and names the first as surfaced; it never decides when the page opens it. The page opens the surfaced file only when the surfaced file changes, so the user's own clicks win until a different file is surfaced.

31. **One idle limit.** (Fix round, QA 2026-11-22.) The 30 minutes in commitments 18 and 23 is one constant, `SESSION_IDLE_LIMIT_MS` in `src/util/sessionIdleLimit`, used by `event`'s `judgeLiveness` and `status`'s `foldSessions`; neither declares its own. Util holds only the number; each module keeps its own rule. (Amended 2026-11-25, observer pass, mandatory observer: the constant becomes the quiet limit, 15 minutes, used only to add "quiet, maybe stuck" (commitment 76); it never ends or un-lives a session. Name is the architect's (e.g. `SESSION_QUIET_LIMIT_MS`); util holds only the number.)

32. **An unreadable session is never deleted.** (Fix round, QA 2026-11-22.) When a session's log exists but cannot be read, its liveness cannot be judged, so `event/delete` refuses it with the outcome `unreadable` (distinct from `live`) and removes nothing; the observer answers 409 saying the file could not be read.

33. **Fixed port with an environment override.** (Added 2026-11-23, one observer per machine.) The observer binds `127.0.0.1` on built-in default port `24680`, overridable by the environment variable `AIDE_OBSERVER_PORT`; the view command's `--port` is an optional override too. Port precedence and invalid values: commitment 39. A port held by another program means no start and a reply naming the port; never a fallback port. Both values may be changed at plan time if the architect finds a reason. (Amended 2026-11-25, observer pass, mandatory observer: superseded by commitment 73: no single fixed port; a busy port moves the observer to the next port on the list, and only an exhausted list is a blocker.)

34. **Single instance and version replace.** (Added 2026-11-23.) A start (tool or view command) reads the root `observer.json` and probes `GET /` with its token. No live observer → start normally. Live observer with `version` equal to or newer than the caller's → start nothing, return the link (commitment 26). Live observer older than the caller → the caller sends the token-protected shutdown request (Host check + token, same as posts), waits for that pid to exit, then starts on the same port; the old observer removes `observer.json` only if it still names itself. An older caller never replaces a newer observer. Versions compare as semver of the AIDE package version. An old observer that does not exit: commitment 37. Nothing is lost: events are on disk and the new observer replays them (commitment 10). (Amended 2026-11-25, observer pass, mandatory observer: the version replace starts the newer observer on the port the old one held when it is free after the old one exits, else walks the list (open question). Probe and shutdown use the port in the record.) (Settled 2026-11-25: reuse the old port when free; commitment 83.)

35. **Project listing comes from `event/read`.** (Added 2026-11-23; orchestrator recommendation recorded.) `event/read` lists projects: each project folder under the app-data root that holds at least one session, with the project root its newest session's `session_started` reported, and that project's sessions with the per-session live flag (commitment 18). The observer serves this list directly as the home view. `status` stays per project and is unchanged. (Amended 2026-11-25, observer pass, mandatory observer: each listed session carries its commitment-76 liveness instead of a `live` flag.)

36. **Per-session view confined to its project.** (Added 2026-11-23.) A session's view (tree, board, status, history) is built by `status` from that session's project: files from the project root its newest session reported, board per commitment 12, events of that project's sessions. Every file read for a view is resolved inside that one project root and refused outside it, however the path is written; a view never reads another project's files or board.

37. **Old observer that does not exit.** (User, 2026-11-23.) If the older observer's pid is still alive a few seconds after the shutdown request of commitment 34, the caller does not start a new one and does not kill the old one; its reply carries the old observer's version and link, so the agent tells the user once and the pipeline carries on. (Amended 2026-11-25, observer pass, mandatory observer: an old observer that does not exit is still a running observer, so the session is observed and proceeds (no blocker); its version and link are reported once as before.)

38. **Project with no reported root.** (User, 2026-11-23.) A project folder whose sessions never reported `projectRoot` is still listed on the home view (commitment 35) with its sessions and history; its tree and board show as unavailable with the reason.

39. **Port precedence and invalid values.** (User, 2026-11-23.) The view command's `--port` beats `AIDE_OBSERVER_PORT`, which beats the `24680` default. A non-integer or out-of-range value refuses the start and the reply names the bad value; never a silent fallback to the next source. (Amended 2026-11-25, observer pass, mandatory observer: precedence now orders the head of the port list (commitment 73): `--port` (view command) beats `AIDE_OBSERVER_PORT`; either, when set, is tried first and then the list. An invalid value still refuses the start by name.)

40. **View command link target.** (User, 2026-11-23.) The view command prints the link to the newest session view of the project it was run from, or to the home view when that project has no sessions.

41. **Posts carry the project key.** (Architect, plan 2026-11-23.) The recorder sends its project key (commitment 19) in the header `protocol.projectHeader` (`x-aide-observer-project`); the post body is unchanged and strict. The observer answers 400 when the header is missing or fails `util/runtimePaths/isProjectKey`, and 409 when the key differs from the key the session was first held under. The store files each session under its key.

42. **Home list built with `event`'s rules.** (Architect, plan 2026-11-23.) (Reading of commitment 35.) `event/read` seeds the store at replay and posts keep it current. `GET /api/projects` answers from the store. Each project's root comes from `event`'s `shared/findNewestStart` (the newest `session_started`, never merged with older ones); each live flag comes from `judgeLiveness`.

43. **Shutdown request.** (Architect, plan 2026-11-23.) `POST /api/shutdown` with the token header and a strict JSON body `{ version }`. 403 when any `Origin` header is present; 415 when the body is not JSON; 409 unless the caller's version is strictly newer (`util/isVersionBehind`); otherwise 202, after which the observer stops and removes the record only if it still names it. If either version cannot be parsed, nothing is replaced.

44. **Port settings.** (Architect, plan 2026-11-23.) `protocol.defaultPort` is `24680` and `protocol.portEnvVar` is `"AIDE_OBSERVER_PORT"`. `src/observer/resolvePort` applies the commitment-39 precedence for both the tool and the view command. A blank env value counts as unset. (Amended 2026-11-25, observer pass, mandatory observer: `protocol.defaultPort` becomes the list `protocol.ports` = 24680..24689 (commitment 73); `portEnvVar` unchanged.)

45. **Versions.** (Architect, plan 2026-11-23.) The running version comes from `util/readPackageVersion()` and is compared with `util/isVersionBehind` (moved from `tools/info`). `GET /` answers with the version, and the single-instance probe uses it.

46. **Links and routes.** (Architect, plan 2026-11-23.) Home is `<address>/?token=<token>`. A session view is `<address>/projects/<key>/sessions/<id>?token=<token>`, built by `src/observer/buildViewLink`; the session path answers a JSON placeholder until the UI round. Per-project APIs live under `/api/projects/<key>/`. Intake stays `POST /api/events`. The stream takes an optional `?project=` filter. (Amended 2026-11-23, redesign round, user decision: the session view moves to `<address>/p/<key>/s/<id>?token=<token>`, with the page's own `&tab=tree|plans|board|record|sessions` and `&item=module:…|task:…|file:…|event:…` deep-link parameters (commitment 62); the old `/projects/<key>/sessions/<id>` path is removed with no redirect, since nothing was released. Home, APIs, intake and stream are unchanged.)

47. **Waiting for the old observer.** (Architect, plan 2026-11-23.) (Settles the "few seconds" of commitment 37.) The caller polls the old observer's pid every 100 ms for up to 3 s; it never kills it.

48. **No reported root.** (Architect, plan 2026-11-23.) (Implements commitment 38.) The view answers `{ state: "no-project-root", projectKey, detail }`; the file route answers 409 and the task route 404; the project's sessions and events are still served, and `status` is not called.

49. **`--brain` scope.** (Architect, plan 2026-11-23.) (Implements commitment 12.) The view command passes `{ projectRoot, brainPath }`; the observer applies the brain path only to that root's project key. If the command finds an observer already running, it keeps that observer's setting and prints a note.

50. **History rows for a project with no reported root.** (User, 2026-11-23; settles the architect's open question.) `status` builds the labeled history rows and needs a root, and this round leaves `status` unchanged, so a no-root project's view offers its raw events (`/api/projects/<key>/events`) and session rows, not labeled history rows. Revisit in the UI round if the page needs labeled rows.

51. **The page is a bundled React app.** (Architect, UI plan 2026-11-23.) `src/observer/page/index.tsx` (React 19 + react-dom, plus the pure browser-safe helpers it imports) is bundled by esbuild at `npm run build` into `dist/observer/assets/page.js` and `page.css`, the JetBrains Mono woff2 files inlined into `page.css` as data URIs, the OFL licence shipped beside them. (Amended 2026-11-23, redesign round: Inter (`@fontsource/inter`, dev dependency, user-approved; weights 400/500/600/700/800, latin + latin-ext) is inlined the same way beside JetBrains Mono 400–700, its licence shipped as `Inter-OFL.txt`; `buildPage` fails unless both families' `@font-face` rules and both licences are present.) No CDN, no runtime Babel, no request beyond the local observer. `tsc` never emits the page (excluded from `tsconfig.build.json`).

52. **HTML shell routes.** (Architect, UI plan 2026-11-23; supersedes the JSON placeholder in #46.) `GET /projects/<key>/sessions/<id>` answers the HTML shell (amended 2026-11-23: now `GET /p/<key>/s/<id>`, commitment 46; the shell's `theme-color` becomes `#0D1117`, the handoff's dark `--bg`). `GET /` answers the same shell when the request's `Accept` lists `text/html`, else the unchanged JSON the single-instance probe reads; both carry `Vary: Accept`. The shell references `/assets/page.css?token=<t>` and `/assets/page.js?token=<t>` (module script), has no inline script or style and no external URL. `GET /assets/<name>` serves exactly `page.js` and `page.css` (any other name 404; a missing bundle 503 naming `npm run build`). Shell and assets are not read routes (they answer during the replay). Token, Host and Origin rules of #26 apply to all of them. (Amended 2026-11-24, user decision: a bad or expired token must reach the handoff's calm "This link is no longer valid" screen, not a raw JSON 401. So the shell (`GET /` when `Accept` lists `text/html`, and `GET /p/<key>/s/<id>`) and `GET /assets/<name>` for the allowlisted names are served WITHOUT the token; they hold no project data. The Host and Origin checks still apply to them. `GET /` without `text/html` (the JSON probe) keeps the token. The shell's asset references drop the token (`/assets/page.css`, `/assets/page.js`), so the shell echoes no unvalidated input at all; the page reads its token from its own URL.)

53. **Page response headers.** (Architect, UI plan 2026-11-23.) HTML: `Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self'; font-src data:; img-src 'self' data:; connect-src 'self'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'`. Every page answer: `Cache-Control: no-store`, `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`, `Referrer-Policy: no-referrer`, no `Access-Control-*`. Content types `text/html; charset=utf-8`, `text/javascript; charset=utf-8`, `text/css; charset=utf-8`.

54. **One token per user across restarts.** (Architect, UI plan 2026-11-23; user-approved 2026-11-23; amends #9's "fresh random secret per observer start".) The token lives in `<app-data root>/observer-token` (64 lowercase hex), created once (exclusive create, mode 0600) and reused by every later observer start; a file holding anything else is replaced with a fresh token. `observer.json` still carries it. An open page and every printed link stay valid across a restart or a version replace.

55. **Page data flow.** (Architect, UI plan 2026-11-23.) A session view subscribes `GET /api/stream?project=<key>&token=<t>` and gets the snapshot then a fresh `status` frame; Home subscribes unfiltered and loads `GET /api/projects` and each project's `GET /api/projects/<key>/status` once at load. The page keeps held events deduped on `(sessionId, seq)` in seq order, replaces a project's view on each `status` frame and the lists on `sessions` / `projects` frames, and reads files, tasks and the delete through the JSON API with the token header. On a stream error it shows `reconnecting`, probes a JSON route every 2 s, reopens on 200, and shows not-found on 404 and an invalid-link notice on 401. (Amended 2026-11-24: since the shell now loads with any token (commitment 52), the page calls the API with whatever token its URL carries, including none; a 401 from the stream or any first API read renders `InvalidLink` with no connection pill, rail or data, and never a reconnecting state.)

56. **Rail on the page.** (Architect, UI plan 2026-11-23.) The page lays `status.rail` out as the export does (main Spec, Research, Synthesize, Plan, Plan approval, Build; loop QA, Fix; Done; lanes Structure, Align, Refactor, Cleanup). (Amended 2026-11-23, redesign round: the handoff draws the nine as one row of `li.av-ph` cells with states `todo | run | wait | done | fail`, a `round <r>` loop badge on Fix, and per-cell meta text built from reported events by the page plan's step 7; lanes take `todo | run | done | fail`.) Plan approval and Done come only from reported events: approval `wait` from a waiting plan file of the rail module, `done` from a `gate_resolved` naming that plan in the rail session; Done from a `qa_verdict passed` for the module in the rail session; QA `fail` from a newest `qa_verdict issues`. No `skipped` state (no event reports one). (Amended 2026-11-25, observer pass, mandatory observer: the rail is session-scoped and names its pipeline (commitment 80). Cells take `done | skipped | run | wait | todo | fail`; `skipped` comes only from a `phase_skipped` report, and the "No `skipped` state" rule above is withdrawn. A working session with no phase running whose latest reports are `file_written` or `commit` shows "Working outside the pipeline" instead of a running cell. Liveness on the page reads working / waiting / quiet N min, maybe stuck / ended.)

57. **No-root history on the page.** (Architect, UI plan 2026-11-23; settles #50's "revisit in the UI round".) For a project with no reported root the page labels the held events with `status`'s own pure `buildHistory`, imported into the bundle, so labels are identical to a ready project's; `status` itself is unchanged. (Superseded 2026-11-23, redesign round: the page composes every history row, rooted or not, from the held events' own fields in the handoff's wording (page plan step 10); it no longer reads `status.history` or imports `buildHistory`, only its `formatDuration`. `status` is unchanged.)

58. **Copy sources.** (Architect, UI plan 2026-11-23; user-approved 2026-11-23.) Static copy is the export's, verbatim; data text (history labels, phase labels, reasons, details, hints) is the observer's; data the observer does not report is omitted (the `claude-code` client name, the session record's line count, a skipped phase, a `Session ended` row). New copy for states the export does not draw (breadcrumb, reconnecting, Home, not-found, invalid link, board-unavailable variants) is listed in the page plan's Decisions and approved as written. The export's not-installed sentence "The viewer watches for the folder and refreshes on its own." is dropped and its `npx aidemd-mcp init` line replaced with the package's real install command; the board-unavailable block keeps the export's `✕ <what> — board can't load` shape with per-reason headings and shows the observer's detail and hint instead of the export's MCP/Obsidian sentences and retry line. (Amended 2026-11-23, redesign round: the source is now the new handoff, `design/aide-viewer-handoff/` (README + `Viewer.dc.html`), copied verbatim for static copy; history labels follow the handoff's wording composed from event fields (#57); the not-installed and Home-empty command box (one box) shows `npx -y @aidemd-mcp/server@latest init`, built from `aidePackage.name` and never typed by hand (user, 2026-11-23: it works on a fresh machine because init installs the server globally, session decision #56), not the handoff's assumed `npx @aidemd-mcp/server init` and not `aidemd-mcp init`; the board error's retry line states the real behaviour, `Read again on every new event and page load · last try HH:MM:SS`, never a timer; the new copy for undrawn states is listed in `src/observer/page/plan.aide` Decisions and was user-approved as written 2026-11-23.)

59. **QA fixture observer.** (Architect, UI plan 2026-11-23.) `src/observer/seedFixtures` writes fixture projects, boards and session logs mirroring the export's scenarios into a caller-chosen app-data root; `npm run observer:fixtures -- --port <n>` (default 24799, 24680 refused) runs an observer on a temp app-data root with them and prints the links (amended 2026-11-23: the scenarios' data is re-sourced from the new handoff's `Viewer.dc.html`, and `--set sessions|home|home-many` picks the scenario set: the handoff's session and edge states, its five-project Home, or Home with twelve projects; `--empty` gives an empty Home; reconnecting is produced by restarting the fixture observer, an invalid link by a wrong token); nothing touches the user's real observer. Screenshots go to `qa-artifacts/` (git-ignored).

60. **Known visual differences from the export.** (User-accepted 2026-11-23. Superseded 2026-11-23, redesign round, by commitment 66; kept for reference.) QA's visual pass treats these as expected, not drift: (a) data the observer does not report is absent — the `claude-code` client name, the session record's line count, a `skipped` phase state, `Session ended` rows, the `Done · <module>` history row; (b) Plan approval and Done rail cells fill only from reported events (#56), so a phase the design shows as skipped reads `not started`; (c) history labels are the status builder's wording (e.g. `Waiting on you: plan.aide`, `Session started`), except finished-phase rows, which the page composes to match the export's duration styling; (d) the `idle` fixture shows about 40 minutes without events (the observer's idle rule needs over 30), not the export's 14m; (e) the `noEvents` fixture holds one `session_started` event and its history row, since the observer cannot list a project with no session; (f) the corrected copy of #58 and the new copy for undrawn states (breadcrumb, reconnecting, stand-in Home, not-found, invalid link, unreadable chip, empty-section text). Everything else (tokens, layout, static copy, interactions at 1440×900 and 375×812, dark and light) must match.

61. **Stylesheet byte copy.** (Architect, redesign plan 2026-11-23; replaces the pv.css byte copy of the first UI round.) `src/observer/page/styles/viewer/index.css` is a byte-for-byte copy of `design/aide-viewer-handoff/design_reference/viewer.css`, pinned by a test. Local rules live only in `styles/additions/index.css`: `body{margin:0}` and the skip link under `.av`. (Amended 2026-11-24, user decision: plus one phone-only rule, scoped `.av.phone`, giving interactive controls a 44px minimum touch target, because the README asks for ≥ 44px and `viewer.css` only does it for `.av-btn`; `viewer.css` stays a byte copy.) `styles/pv/` and its test are retired, as is `design/observer-page/design_reference` as a reference.

62. **Page URL state.** (Architect, redesign plan 2026-11-23.) The page parses `/p/<key>/s/<id>` plus `tab` (`tree` default, `plans`, `board`, `record`, `sessions`, `history`; unknown → `tree`) and `item` (`module:<folder>` with `.` for the root, `task:<slug>`, `file:<project-relative path>`, `event:<sessionId>:<seq>`; anything else → no drawer). Changing tab or opening or closing the drawer pushes a history entry; Back restores it. The live-session switcher does not change the URL. One builder (`page/route/buildHref`) writes every page link, with the same path shape as `buildViewLink`.

63. **Theme and phone mode.** (Architect, redesign plan 2026-11-23.) The page root is `<div class="av t-dark|t-light phone|desk tab-<tab>">`. First load follows `prefers-color-scheme`; a toggle persists under localStorage key `aide-observer-theme`. `phone` is set while `matchMedia("(max-width: 760px)")` matches.

64. **Document title.** (Architect, redesign plan 2026-11-23.) Base `AIDE Pipeline Viewer` from `src/observer/shared/pageTitle`; a session view is `<project> — AIDE Pipeline Viewer`; while a gate is open (any project on Home, the project on a session view) the title starts `Waiting on you — `.

65. **Drawer.** (Architect, redesign plan 2026-11-23; user-approved 2026-11-23.) Non-modal as the handoff draws it (`role="dialog" aria-modal="false"`, overlaying the right side, 540px, full screen on phone), labelled by its visible title, closed by Esc and its close button, focus moved in on open and back to the opener on close. Content is fetched per item through the JSON API; a failed read shows the observer's message inside the drawer only.

66. **Known visual differences from the handoff.** (Architect, redesign plan 2026-11-23; user-accepted 2026-11-23; replaces #60.) QA's visual pass treats these as expected, not drift: (a) data the observer does not report is absent: client name, skipped phase state, line counts, the fix card's "Round 1 fixed 3 issues" clause, the idle card's "then"/"Finished" lines, the history `Done` row and the "Connection to the viewer server lost" row; (b) a task move shows `from → to` only when an earlier event reported the from-column, else `→ to`; (c) the `s-idle` fixture shows about 40 minutes without events (idle needs over 30), not the handoff's 12m; (d) the `s-no-events` fixture holds `session_started` and `prompt` (the observer cannot list a project with no session); (e) the install command and the board retry line of #58; (f) the new copy for undrawn states listed in the page plan's Decisions; (g) values relative to now (elapsed, `2m ago`) differ by the run time. Everything else (tokens, type, spacing, layout, static copy, states, interactions at 1440×900 and 375×812, dark and light) must match.

67. **Data routes always require the token.** (User decision 2026-11-24.) Only the page shell (`GET /` with `Accept: text/html`, `GET /p/<key>/s/<id>`) and `GET /assets/<allowlisted name>` answer without the token. Every other route requires it exactly as before: `GET /` as JSON, `/api/projects`, `/api/projects/<key>/*` (status, events, file, task, sessions), `DELETE /api/projects/<key>/sessions/<id>`, `/api/stream`, `POST /api/events`, `POST /api/shutdown`. The Host check and the Origin rules apply to every request, tokenless ones included. A test pins each tokenless route and that every data route answers 401 without the token.

68. **Pipelines in the event schema (v2).** (Observer pass, user 2026-11-25.) Schema version `v: 2`. A pipeline is one feature flow run by the /aide orchestrator from the stage it starts at to completion (cleanup done) or abandonment, across every module it plans. Its id is the kanban task slug when the flow has a task, else a short name the agent gives (bounded label, commitment 16 bounds). New kinds: `pipeline_started` (pipeline, phase: the starting phase); `pipeline_finished` (pipeline, result `completed | abandoned`); `phase_skipped` (pipeline, phase, reason: bounded string). `phase_started`, `phase_finished`, `gate_waiting`, `gate_resolved`, `plan_step_checked` and `qa_verdict` gain a required `pipeline` in v2. Every skip is reported (a flow starting later than spec, research or synthesize skipped by routing, QA passing with no fix loop). Hand work (`file_written`, `commit` outside any phase) names no pipeline. The envelope (`v`, `ts`, `sessionId`, `seq`, `pid`) is unchanged. v1 lines (no `pipeline`) still replay and read; they belong to no pipeline. A later session resuming the same task continues the same pipeline id.

69. **HALT reply of `aide_observer_start`.** (Observer pass, user 2026-11-25.) After the observer is up, start lists the project's sessions other than the caller's whose liveness (commitment 76) is not `ended`, judged from presence and ended marks on disk via `event`, never from the running observer's store. If any, the reply is HALT and names, per such session: `sessionId`, state `working | waiting`, quiet minutes with "maybe stuck" when past the quiet limit, last activity time, and its current pipeline and phase from its log (absent when it reported none). The reply text says it is a hard stop: nothing proceeds until the user approves ending that session with `aide_observer_end_session` or closes this one. The caller's own session and ended sessions never cause a HALT. The HALT reply still carries the link so the user can look. Exact field names and wording are the architect's. (Amended 2026-11-25, user: also counts unlinked presence; see commitment 82.)

70. **No-port blocker.** (Observer pass, user 2026-11-25.) When the observer can bind no port on the list (commitment 73), whether at session start or on the one restart of commitment 21, `aide_observer_start` replies with a blocker naming every port tried and why each failed; the agent tells the user and nothing proceeds. Distinct from HALT and from the old-observer reply of commitment 37.

71. **Hook session ↔ observer session link.** (Observer pass, user 2026-11-25.) Claude Code identifies a session by the hook input's `session_id`; the observer by its own `sessionId` (commitment 20). The `session_started` event reply names the observer `sessionId` in a parseable form. The hook on PostToolUse for that `aide_observer_event` call reads `tool_input.kind == "session_started"` and the `tool_response`, extracts the `sessionId` and writes it into that Claude session's presence. From then on presence is found by observer `sessionId`. Presence recorded before the link (SessionStart, the first prompt) is kept under the Claude session id and counts once linked. The exact reply token the hook parses is the architect's, owned by `event`'s shared protocol so the tool and the hook cannot drift. (Amended 2026-11-25, user: unlinked presence still counts toward HALT, named by its Claude session id; commitment 82.)

72. **Presence and ended-mark files.** (Observer pass, user 2026-11-25.) Under `<root>/<project-key>/` (commitment 7), beside `events/` and never inside a log: one presence file per Claude session, holding `{ v, claudeSessionId, sessionId?, state: working | waiting | ended, at (hook time, ISO UTC), hookEvent, transcriptPath? }`, replaced atomically (write temp, rename) on every hook call, newest `at` wins when two calls race; and one ended mark per observer session, `{ v, sessionId, endedAt, endedBy: "hook" | "user", bySessionId? }`, written once and never removed except by delete (commitment 13). SessionEnd writes presence `ended` and the ended mark; `aide_observer_end_session` writes only the ended mark. `transcriptPath` comes from the hook's `transcript_path`; when a hook input lacks it, the last path seen is kept. The project key comes from the hook input's `cwd` resolved the same way as commitment 19. Paths come from `util/runtimePaths`; folder and file names are the architect's.

    *Amendment 2026-11-25 (architect, fix loop, src/cli/hook/todo.aide; event plan step 8):* presence is no longer replaced on every hook call. A call rewrites the file (and then pings, commitment 99) only when it changes `state`, `sessionId` or `transcriptPath`, or when its time is at least `PRESENCE_REFRESH_MS` = 30 000 ms after the stored `at` (`event/touchPresence/shouldRefresh`); any other call writes nothing and pings nothing. `at` therefore means "time of the last refresh", at most about 30 s behind the last hook call during continuous activity. Newest `at` still wins a race. The ended-mark step is unaffected.

73. **Port list.** (Observer pass, user 2026-11-25; replaces commitment 33.) The observer tries, in order: the user-set port if any (`--port` for the view command, else `AIDE_OBSERVER_PORT`, precedence of commitment 39), then 24680, 24681, … 24689. It binds the first free one and records it in `observer.json` (commitment 9). A port held by this user's own observer is not "taken": start reuses that observer (commitment 34). Still one observer per user per machine. The QA fixture observer's 24799 (commitment 59) stays outside the list.

74. **End-session tool.** (Observer pass, user 2026-11-25.) `aide_observer_end_session { sessionId }` writes the ended mark of commitment 72 with `endedBy: "user"` and `bySessionId` = the caller's session, for another session of the caller's own project. Refused (nothing written) for the caller's own session, an unknown session, or a session of another project. Already ended → succeeds, unchanged. Deletes nothing, ever. The agent calls it only after the user approves (aide-observer skill prose); the tool cannot check that.

75. **Presence ping route.** (Observer pass, user 2026-11-25.) After writing presence, `aidemd-mcp hook` reads `observer.json` and, if an observer runs, posts a ping to the route `protocol.presenceRoute` with the token and project header (commitment 41) and a strict body naming the Claude session id. Short cap (same order as commitment 5's ~250 ms), every failure swallowed. The hook never starts the observer, prints nothing for a person, and always exits 0.

76. **Liveness model.** (Observer pass, user 2026-11-25; replaces commitments 18 and 23.) `event`'s `judgeLiveness` returns per session `{ state: working | waiting | ended, lastActivityAt, quietMinutes, maybeStuck, endedBy? }`. Rules: an ended mark → `ended` (with `endedBy`); else the last hook state of the linked presence (`ended` from a SessionEnd counts as ended); else, for a session with no presence (host without hooks), `working`. `lastActivityAt` = newest of presence `at`, the transcript file's last-write time (`readTranscriptActivity`) and the newest event `ts`. `maybeStuck` when now − `lastActivityAt` exceeds the 15-minute quiet limit (commitment 31). Nothing ends a session automatically; no pid, no process start time, no heartbeat, no reliance on the aide MCP server.

77. **Hooks installed by the package.** (Observer pass, user 2026-11-25.) A new `hooks` category for init and upgrade merges only aide's own entries into the project's `.claude/settings.json` (Claude Code only): SessionStart, UserPromptSubmit, PostToolUse, Stop and SessionEnd each run `aidemd-mcp hook`. Every other byte of the file is left unchanged; upgrade's merge is consent-gated. Needs a `[migration]` entry (Detect: a Claude Code project whose `.claude/settings.json` lacks aide's hook entries; Confirm: once, as a batch). The `aide-observer` skill ships as the seventh skill.

78. **Hook events map.** (Observer pass, user 2026-11-25.) SessionStart, UserPromptSubmit, PostToolUse → `working`; Stop → `waiting`; SessionEnd → `ended` plus the ended mark when the Claude session is linked. Any other or malformed input is ignored (exit 0).

79. **Resuming a pipeline across sessions.** (Observer pass, user 2026-11-25.) `src/observer` hands `status` the events of every kept session of the project (live, ended and archived; a deleted session is gone and forgotten). On the live session's current pipeline X, `status` marks done each stage that an earlier session's events for X finished (a `phase_finished`, or a later phase of X started, or a gate of X resolved). There is no separate pipeline record; the logs are the pipeline state.

80. **Session-scoped rail rules.** (Observer pass, user 2026-11-25.) Only a live session has a rail. It shows its current pipeline (newest `pipeline_started` without a later `pipeline_finished`), else the last one it finished until another starts or the session ends. Stages: every stage of the full list, each `done`, `skipped`, `running` or `pending`. Within the pipeline a later phase starting or a gate resolving marks earlier stages done; a running phase ends at the next phase of that pipeline, a `commit`, or the session's end. `skipped` only from `phase_skipped`. With no phase running and the session's latest events `file_written` or `commit`, the rail reads "Working outside the pipeline" (no new event kind). The rail never mixes another pipeline's or a deleted session's stages. (Settled 2026-11-25: an ended session has no rail; commitment 86.)

81. **Observer process never interprets project config.** (Observer pass, user 2026-11-25.) `src/observer` reads no `.aide/config/*`, no instructions file and no settings file; liveness comes from `event`, the board path from the newest `session_started` (commitment 12).

82. **Every open Claude session counts toward HALT.** (User, 2026-11-25; amends commitment 69, answers the unlinked-presence question.) The HALT check counts every presence in the caller's project whose session is not ended (commitment 76), linked to an observer session or not, i.e. whether or not it ever reported `session_started`. An unlinked one is named by its Claude session id, with its state, quiet minutes and last activity (presence `at` and transcript last write); it has no pipeline or phase. The caller's own session never counts: its own presence is recognised by the link of commitment 71 and, before the link lands, by the caller's Claude session id when the tool can learn it (architect to settle how the start tool identifies the caller's own unlinked presence).

83. **A replacing observer reuses the old port.** (User, 2026-11-25; settles commitment 34's open question.) After the older observer exits, the newer one binds the port the old one held when it is free, so open links keep working; only if it is taken does it walk the list of commitment 73.

84. **Hookless sessions still halt.** (User, 2026-11-25.) A session with no presence (host without hooks) and no ended mark is `working` by commitment 76 however long it is quiet, shows "quiet N min, maybe stuck", and causes a HALT like any other open session until the user approves ending it.

85. **No start lock.** (User, 2026-11-25.) Two sessions starting together may both pass the HALT check before either's presence lands; this is accepted. No lock file or project-level start lock.

86. **A just-ended session has no rail.** (User, 2026-11-25; settles commitment 80's session-end case.) Only live sessions have a rail; once a session is ended its rail is hidden, not shown with its phase closed.

87. **Claude Code hook inputs.** (Architect, plan 2026-11-25; settles the transcript_path question, source: code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks.) SessionStart, UserPromptSubmit, PostToolUse, Stop and SessionEnd inputs all carry `session_id`, `transcript_path`, `cwd` and `hook_event_name`; SessionStart adds `source` (`startup | resume | clear | compact | fork`), SessionEnd adds `reason` (`clear | resume | logout | prompt_input_exit | other`), PostToolUse adds `tool_name` (MCP tools `mcp__<server>__<tool>`, plugin servers `mcp__plugin_<plugin>_<server>__<tool>`), `tool_input`, `tool_response`. The MCP `tool_response` shape is undocumented, so the hook searches the JSON text of the whole `tool_response` for the session tag (commitment 90), never a fixed path. Presence still keeps the last `transcriptPath` seen when an input lacks it (commitment 72's rule stays as a fallback). Plain stdout of a SessionStart or UserPromptSubmit hook becomes model context, so `aidemd-mcp hook` writes nothing to stdout or stderr, ever. Matching hooks run in parallel and PostToolUse fires once per tool call (concurrently for parallel calls). SessionEnd hooks have a 1.5 s default timeout, so the hook process exits by a 1 000 ms hard deadline whatever is pending.

88. **Hooks settings entries.** (Architect, plan 2026-11-25.) The `hooks` category merges into `<project>/.claude/settings.json`, under the top-level `hooks` object, one matcher group per event: `SessionStart`, `UserPromptSubmit`, `Stop`, `SessionEnd` each get `{ "hooks": [ { "type": "command", "command": "aidemd-mcp hook" } ] }` (no `matcher`, which means all), and `PostToolUse` gets `{ "matcher": "*", "hooks": [ { "type": "command", "command": "aidemd-mcp hook" } ] }`. No `timeout` key. The command is built from `install/shared/aidePackage.command` + `" hook"`, never typed. An event counts as already wired when any matcher group in its array holds a hook of `type: "command"` whose trimmed `command` equals that string. A merge appends only the missing groups; every other byte of the file is unchanged.

89. **The hook's project root.** (Architect, plan 2026-11-25; amends commitment 72's "from the hook input's cwd".) The hook resolves the project root from the environment variable `CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR` when set and non-blank (Claude Code sets it for hooks and stdio MCP servers to the root the session started in, the same folder the aide server records under), else from the input's `cwd`. The key is then computed by `util/runtimePaths/resolve` exactly as commitment 19, so a `cd` inside the session never moves presence to another project.

90. **Session tag in every event reply.** (Architect, plan 2026-11-25; settles commitment 71's reply token, amends it.) Every `aide_observer_event` reply, whatever its outcome, ends with one space and the tag `[observer-session <sessionId>]`. The label `observer-session` is `protocol.sessionTagLabel`; `event/shared/formatSessionTag(sessionId) → string` builds the tag and `event/shared/parseSessionTag(text) → string | null` finds the first tag in any text and returns its id only when `util/runtimePaths/shared/isSessionId` accepts it. The hook links on PostToolUse of any tool whose name ends with `__aide_observer_event` and whose `tool_response` JSON text carries a tag, not only on `session_started` (any event reply links, idempotently), so a missed link is repaired by the next report.

91. **How start recognises the caller's own session.** (Architect, plan 2026-11-25; settles commitment 82's open part.) A PostToolUse hook's result is attached to the tool result the model receives, so the hook has finished before the model can read that result and issue a call that depends on it. The boot calls are sequential (start needs nothing from the parallel world, and session_started's reply is read first), so the caller's presence is linked before `aide_observer_start` runs. As a second guard, start waits, polling every 100 ms for at most 1 000 ms, while non-ended unlinked presence exists in the project and no presence links to the caller; then it runs the HALT check with whatever is on disk. The caller's own observer session and every presence linked to it never count. The aide-observer skill (prose) must say: call `session_started` alone, read its reply, never batch it with another call. Residual risk: a caller whose hook failed to link appears in its own HALT under its Claude session id; the HALT names it and the user can recognise it.

92. **`/clear` and `/resume` do not end the observer session.** (Architect, plan 2026-11-25; refines commitment 78.) The MCP server process, and with it the observer session, outlives a `/clear` or `/resume` inside one Claude Code process, while the Claude session id changes. So SessionEnd with `reason` `clear` or `resume` writes that Claude session's presence `ended` but no ended mark; any other reason writes presence `ended` and, when the presence is linked, the ended mark with `endedBy: "hook"`. The new Claude session links to the same observer session on its next report (commitment 90). An observer session's liveness reads its newest-`at` linked presence.

93. **Ending an unlinked presence.** (Architect, plan 2026-11-25; extends commitment 74 so a HALT on a never-reporting session can be released.) `aide_observer_end_session { sessionId }` also accepts the Claude session id of a presence in the caller's project. When that presence links to an observer session, that observer session is ended; when it is unlinked, an ended mark keyed by the Claude session id is written (`claudeSessionId` instead of `sessionId` in the mark). Liveness of an unlinked presence honours that mark. Own-session refusal covers the caller's observer id and every Claude id linked to it.

94. **Logs from before hooks stay live.** (User, 2026-11-25: "No, keep them live"; the architect's `legacy` proposal is withdrawn.) A session with no linked presence and no ended mark is judged by commitment 76 whatever its schema version: a v1 session reads `working`, quiet and maybe stuck once past the quiet limit, and counts toward HALT (commitment 84) until the user ends it with `aide_observer_end_session`. There is no `legacy` value anywhere.

95. **Presence and ended-mark files.** (Architect, plan 2026-11-25; settles commitment 72's names.) Presence: `<root>/<project-key>/presence/<claudeSessionId>.json`. Ended marks: `<root>/<project-key>/ended/<id>.json`, `<id>` being the observer session id or, for commitment 93, the Claude session id. Both ids must pass `isSessionId`. `util/runtimePaths/shared/runtimeNames` adds `PRESENCE_FOLDER = "presence"`, `ENDED_FOLDER = "ended"`, `RECORD_EXTENSION = ".json"`; `RuntimePaths` and `ProjectFolder` gain `presenceFolder` and `endedFolder`; `create` makes events, presence and ended folders; new pure resolvers `resolvePresenceFile({ projectKey, claudeSessionId })` and `resolveEndedMark({ projectKey, id })`. Presence schema (`event/shared/presenceRecord`, named `schema`, strict): `{ v: 1, claudeSessionId, sessionId?, state: "working" | "waiting" | "ended", at: ISO datetime, hookEvent: "SessionStart" | "UserPromptSubmit" | "PostToolUse" | "Stop" | "SessionEnd", transcriptPath?: path ≤ 1024 }`. Ended mark schema (`event/shared/endedMark`, named `schema`, strict): `{ v: 1, sessionId?, claudeSessionId?, endedAt: ISO datetime, endedBy: "hook" | "user", bySessionId? }`, exactly one of `sessionId` / `claudeSessionId` present. A mark is created exclusively (`wx`); an existing mark is left as it is.

96. **Liveness shape and API.** (Architect, plan 2026-11-25; implements commitment 76.) Type `SessionLiveness = { state: "working" | "waiting" | "ended"; lastActivityAt: string | null; quietMinutes: number; maybeStuck: boolean; endedBy?: "hook" | "user" }`. `event/shared/judgeLiveness({ events, presence, endedMark, transcriptActivityAt, now }) → SessionLiveness` is pure: an ended mark → `ended` with its `endedBy`; else presence `ended` → `ended`, `endedBy: "hook"`; else presence state; else `working` (any schema version, commitment 94). `lastActivityAt` = newest of presence `at`, `transcriptActivityAt` and newest event `ts` (null when none); `quietMinutes` = whole minutes since it (0 when null); `maybeStuck` = not ended and `now − lastActivityAt > SESSION_QUIET_LIMIT_MS`. The one composed reader is `event/readLiveness({ projectKey, sessions: { sessionId, events }[], now }) → Promise<LivenessReport>`, `LivenessReport = { sessions: Record<sessionId, SessionLiveness>; unlinked: UnlinkedPresence[] }`, `UnlinkedPresence = { claudeSessionId; liveness: SessionLiveness }` (ended ones included; callers filter). `SESSION_QUIET_LIMIT_MS` = 15 × 60 000, default export of `src/util/sessionQuietLimit`; `src/util/sessionIdleLimit` is retired.

97. **Exact tool replies.** (Architect, plan 2026-11-25; settles the wording commitments 5, 69, 70 and 74 leave to the architect.) `aide_observer_event`: `recorded`, `recorded; observer not running`, `not recorded (<detail>)`, `not recorded (<detail>); observer not running`, `not recorded: invalid event (<detail>)`, `session already started; nothing recorded`, `refused: session ended`, each followed by ` [observer-session <id>]`. `aide_observer_start` first line begins with exactly one of `Observer running at <link>` (may add ` (replaced the older observer, version <v>)`), `Observer already running at <link>`, `Observer not replaced: …` (commitment 37, proceed), `HALT: ` or `BLOCKED: `. HALT: line 1 `HALT: another session of this project is open. Nothing proceeds until the user approves ending it with aide_observer_end_session, or closes this session.`, then one line per open session `- <sessionId | claude:<claudeSessionId>> · <working | waiting on the user> · <active | quiet N min, maybe stuck | quiet N min> · last activity <ISO or unknown> · pipeline <id or none> · phase <phase or none>`, then `Observer: <link>`. BLOCKED: `BLOCKED: the observer could not start, so nothing proceeds. <reason>`; for an exhausted list the reason is `No port could be bound: <port> (<why>), …` naming every port tried in order. `aide_observer_end_session`: `ended <id>`, `already ended <id>`, `refused: <id> is this session`, `refused: no session <id> in this project`, `refused: invalid session id`.

    *Amendment 2026-11-25 (architect, fix loop, src/tools/observer/todo.aide):* the `aide_observer_end_session` list gains a sixth reply, `not ended (<detail>)`, for any filesystem read or write failure inside event's `endSession` (which propagates it); `<detail>` is the error's message. Nothing was written when it is returned except possibly the project's folders; no mark exists unless a later call writes one. It is built by the tool's `formatReply` like the other five (tool-local `EndSessionReply` adds `{ outcome: "failed"; detail }`; `EndSessionOutcome` is unchanged). The agent's handling, in the aide-observer skill (prose): tell the user the session was not ended and why; the HALT still stands and nothing proceeds; retry only on the user's say-so.

98. **Port walk.** (Architect, plan 2026-11-25; implements commitments 73 and 83.) `protocol.ports` = `[24680, …, 24689]` replaces `protocol.defaultPort`. `src/observer/resolvePort` returns the head (`--port`, else `AIDE_OBSERVER_PORT`, else `protocol.ports[0]`), and `src/observer/listPorts({ preferred }) → number[]` returns `preferred` then `protocol.ports`, deduplicated, in order. `startObserver({ port })` treats `port` as preferred and binds the first free port of `listPorts`; after a replace, the old observer's port is preferred over everything (commitment 83). The start tool pre-walks the same list with `tryBind` (which now answers why a port failed), spawns `view --port <first free>`, and builds BLOCKED from the per-port reasons when none is free. `observerRecord` adds `port` (int 1–65535), written always but optional on read, because a record written by an older observer has none; readers take the port from `address` then. `RunningObserver` gains `port`.

    *Amendment 2026-11-25 (architect, fix loop, src/cli/todo.aide):* the `view` command reports the walk. When the observer it started or found is not on the port `resolvePort` chose (whatever its source: `--port`, `AIDE_OBSERVER_PORT` or the list head), it prints to stdout, on the line after the link, exactly `Port <requested> was not used; the observer is on port <actual>.` and keeps its exit code (0). It never prints a cause, since `startObserver` returns only the bound port. Only an exhausted list fails the command: `Observer not started: No port could be bound: <port> (<why>), …` on stderr, exit 1. `view --help` describes the list (`--port`, else `AIDE_OBSERVER_PORT`, then the range built from `protocol.ports`). `startObserver`, `resolvePort` and `listPorts` are unchanged. This replaces commitment 33's busy-port exit for `view`.

99. **Presence ping route.** (Architect, plan 2026-11-25; implements commitment 75.) `protocol.presenceRoute` = `/api/presence`. `POST` with the token header, the project header (commitment 41) and a strict JSON body `{ claudeSessionId }` (schema `event/shared/presencePing`, named `schema`); same Host, Origin and content-type rules as `POST /api/events`. Answers 202 and then rebuilds and pushes that project's status and session list; a project the store does not hold still answers 202 and pushes the project list. Cap on the sender 250 ms, every failure swallowed. Sent by `event/touchPresence` after the presence write.

    *Amendment 2026-11-25 (architect, fix loop, src/cli/hook/todo.aide; event plan step 8):* `touchPresence` sends the ping only on a refresh (commitment 72 amendment), never on a quiet call, and `TouchOutcome` gains `pinged: boolean`. The sender is a `node:http` request with `agent: false` (no keep-alive socket) instead of the global `fetch`, which loads its HTTP client lazily on first use and was most of the ~70 ms ping cost; cap, headers, body, route and "every failure swallowed" unchanged. No code on the hook's import path calls the global `fetch` (guarded by the hook's import-graph test).

    *Amendment 2026-11-25 (architect, second fix loop, src/cli/hook/todo.aide; event plan step 9):* on the hook path `node:http` is loaded with `createRequire(import.meta.url)("node:http")`, never a static ESM import, because the ESM namespace of `node:http` loads undici and `worker_threads` (about 38 ms per run). Other modules (the observer server, `record/postToObserver`) keep the ESM import.

100. **Status inputs and rails.** (Architect, plan 2026-11-25; implements commitments 79, 80, 86.) `StatusInput` gains `liveness: Record<sessionId, SessionLiveness>` (one per session in `events`) and `archivedEvents: ObserverEvent[]` (the project's archived sessions, used only to resume pipelines; never in history or sessions). `SessionSummary.liveness` becomes `SessionLiveness`; `SessionSummary.pid` stays; status's own `Liveness` type is removed. `Status.rail` (type `PhaseRail`, removed) becomes `rails: PipelineRail[]`, one per non-ended session, newest-started first. `PipelineRail = { sessionId; pipeline: string | null; pipelineState: "running" | "finished" | "none"; outside: boolean; stages: RailPhase[] }` and `RailPhase = { phase; label; state: "done" | "skipped" | "running" | "pending"; reason?; buildStep?; qaFixRound? }`, every phase of the closed list once in `PHASE_LABELS` order. `pipeline: null, pipelineState: "none"` covers a session that reported no pipeline (v1 or hand work): its stages come from its own pipeline-less phase events. `status/shared/findCurrentPipeline({ events }) → { pipeline: string; state: "running" | "finished" } | null` and `status/shared/findOpenPhase({ events, pipeline }) → PhaseStartedEvent | null` are the one rule for "current pipeline" and "running phase", used by status and by the start tool's HALT. A running phase closes at a later `phase_finished` of the same phase and module, a later `phase_started` of the same pipeline, a `commit` or the pipeline's `pipeline_finished`; Build still needs an approved plan.

101. **Quiet minutes on the page.** (Architect, plan 2026-11-25.) The page re-derives only the displayed quiet minutes from `lastActivityAt` on its own tick, and "maybe stuck" from the same `SESSION_QUIET_LIMIT_MS` (bundled as a pure number), so an open page does not freeze at the minute of the last push. It never re-derives `state`. `util/sessionQuietLimit` joins the bundled pure modules.

102. **Byte-preserving settings merge.** (Architect, plan 2026-11-25; dependency user-approved 2026-11-25.) `src/service/install/shared/planHooksMerge({ text: string | null }) → HooksMergePlan` is the one planner used by install's hooks step and upgrade's hooks category: `{ state: "absent"; merged }` (no file: a new file holding only the hooks object), `{ state: "complete" }`, `{ state: "missing"; events; merged }`, `{ state: "malformed"; reason }`. Edits are computed with `jsonc-parser` (`modify` + `applyEdits`, runtime dependency `jsonc-parser@3.3.1`), which changes only the inserted ranges and keeps the file's indentation and line endings.

103. **Hook speed budget.** (Architect, plan 2026-11-25.) `aidemd-mcp hook` loads only `src/index.ts` → `src/cli/index.ts` → `src/cli/hook` → `event/touchPresence` and what they import (node built-ins, `zod`, `util/runtimePaths`, `event/shared/*`). A test walks the hook's static import graph and fails on `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk`, `ink`, `react`, `yaml`, `@/tools`, `@/observer`, `status` or `kanban`. Budget on Windows, measured once in build: p95 wall time ≤ 300 ms with an observer running, ≤ 300 ms with none; the 1 000 ms deadline is the hard stop.

    *Amendment 2026-11-25 (cli/hook step 3, Windows 11, Node 22, built `dist`, 30 runs each, wall clock of `node dist/index.js hook` fed a tagged PostToolUse `aide_observer_event` reply, temp app-data root and project, fixture observer on port 24799):* observer stopped p50 218-225 ms, p95 253-269 ms (three batches of 30); observer running p50 289-294 ms, p95 316-342 ms (two batches of 30); bare `node -e ""` p50 87 ms, p95 113 ms. The stopped case meets the 300 ms budget; the running case exceeds it at p95 (about 70 ms over the stopped case, spent in the ping's `fetch`). In-process: loading `cli/hook` about 100 ms (zod about 30, `runtimePaths/create` and its chain about 30, `touchPresence` and the rest about 40); a run about 13 ms stopped, about 80 ms with an observer. Import graph and `zod` left unchanged; over-budget case sent to the architect.

    *Amendment 2026-11-25 (architect, fix loop, src/cli/hook/todo.aide; restates the budget):* the absolute 300 ms is retired; it was set before the floor was measured (bare `node` p95 113-121 ms on this machine, and whole-run p95 swinging 253-320 ms between sessions on the same code). Budget now: p95 of `node dist/index.js hook` minus p95 of a bare `node -e ""` batch taken in the same run ≤ 200 ms for a quiet call (no refresh: no write, no ping), observer stopped or running, and ≤ 250 ms for a refreshing call that writes and pings a running observer. The 1 000 ms deadline stays the hard stop. Caught by: (1) the default suite: the import-graph guard (now also failing on a global `fetch` call on the path) and event's tests that a quiet call neither writes nor pings; (2) the opt-in `src/cli/hook/index.speed.test.ts` (`npm run build`, then `AIDE_MEASURE_HOOK=1 npx vitest run src/cli/hook/index.speed.test.ts`), which asserts the budget and is run in QA for any plan touching `src/index.ts`, `src/cli/index.ts`, `src/cli/hook` or `event/touchPresence` and what it imports, and before each publish. A run over budget goes back to the architect before QA; the budget is never left as a known-missed commitment. The numbers are recorded below by cli/hook plan step 4.

    *Amendment 2026-11-25 (architect, second fix loop, src/cli/hook/todo.aide; replaces the budget form of the amendment above):* the build's runs of that budget passed once (quiet overhead 195/190 ms, refreshing 192 ms) and failed once (224/204/240 ms) on the same code, with the bare floor alone moving 136 → 104 ms, so a p95-of-batch bar of 200 ms sat inside the noise. Root cause of the largest avoidable cost: a static ESM import of `node:http` (in `pingObserver`) builds the builtin's ESM namespace, which loads `worker_threads`, the web streams and undici; loading it with `createRequire` avoids that and took the in-process import of `cli/hook` from 148-166 ms to 108-126 ms (event plan step 9). Budget now: each hook run is paired with a bare `node -e ""` run taken right next to it (40 pairs per phase, order alternating); the median of hook minus bare is ≤ 150 ms for a quiet call (observer stopped or running) and ≤ 180 ms for a refreshing call that writes and pings; every single run stays under the 1 000 ms deadline. The p95 is reported, not guaranteed: this loosens the tail promise of the original commitment. A phase whose bare p95 exceeds 2 × its bare p50 is `inconclusive` and rerun, never recorded as pass or fail. Guards: the default suite's import-graph test also fails on a static ESM import of `node:http`/`node:https` on the hook path; the opt-in speed test also checks in a child process that importing the built hook loads no undici, `worker_threads` or web readable streams. What remains (about `zod` 30 ms, ESM resolution 20 ms, `node:crypto` 14 ms, the package's own modules) is not cut further without dropping `zod` from the hook path or bundling the hook entry, neither planned. Numbers are recorded below by cli/hook plan step 5.

    *Amendment 2026-11-25 (cli/hook step 5 run; Windows 11, Node v22.22.0, built `dist`, idle machine, temp app-data root and project, in-process observer on a freed port; `AIDE_MEASURE_HOOK=1 npx vitest run src/cli/hook/index.speed.test.ts`, 40 paired iterations per phase, order alternating, children spawned asynchronously and timed with `process.hrtime.bigint()` so the in-process observer keeps answering pings):* undici check: importing `dist/cli/hook/index.js` in a child printed no `undici`, `worker_threads` or `internal/webstreams/readablestream` entry. Two clean runs, no `inconclusive` (bare p95 under 1.2 × p50 in every phase), every hook run under 1 000 ms, all three phases within budget, presence assertions held (quiet phases wrote nothing, the refreshing phase moved `at` every run). Run 1, ms: (b) observer stopped, quiet: bare p50 92 / p95 99, hook p50 236 / p95 259, difference median 145 / p95 166; (c) observer running, quiet: bare 90 / 105, hook 232 / 254, difference 140 / 155; (d) observer running, refreshing: bare 89 / 104, hook 244 / 267, difference 155 / 179. Run 2, ms: (b) bare 90 / 106, hook 231 / 251, difference 141 / 157; (c) bare 90 / 99, hook 229 / 243, difference 140 / 153; (d) bare 88 / 101, hook 241 / 255, difference 152 / 164. Budget: median difference ≤ 150 ms quiet, ≤ 180 ms refreshing; the quiet medians sit 5-10 ms under the bar, so a regression of about 10 ms on the hook path will fail it. The medians (140-155 ms) are above the architect's 95-100 ms estimate for a quiet call; the undici cost is gone, the rest is the package's own module load named above. The before/after in-process ping timings from event step 8 are not recorded here: they were not passed to this step.

    *Amendment 2026-11-25 (user decision, cli/hook step 1 speed run; restates the budget of the second fix loop):* the paired-median budget is raised to ≤ 175 ms for a quiet call and ≤ 200 ms for a refreshing call (was 150 / 180), until the backlog task `make-the-claude-code-hook-start-faster` lands. Everything else in the second-fix-loop form stands: 40 paired iterations per phase, p95 reported and not asserted, the noise gate (a phase whose bare p95 exceeds 2 × its bare p50 is `inconclusive` and rerun), every run under the 1 000 ms deadline. Reason: with the observer stopped, quiet medians of 152-155 ms against the earlier 150 ms are within this machine's run-to-run noise, and the `AIDEMD_HOME` resolver change (`resolveAppDataRoot`, on the hook's import path) adds no measurable load. Run after the change (Windows 11, Node v22.22.0, built `dist`, `AIDE_MEASURE_HOOK=1 npx vitest run src/cli/hook/index.speed.test.ts`, temp app-data root through `AIDEMD_HOME`, in-process observer on a freed port; 2 tests passed, the undici / `worker_threads` / web-streams check clean, no `inconclusive` phase, every hook run under 1 000 ms), ms: (b) observer stopped, quiet: bare p50 96 / p95 114, hook p50 247 / p95 316, difference median 147 / p95 205; (c) observer running, quiet: bare 94 / 117, hook 240 / 279, difference 145 / 182; (d) observer running, refreshing: bare 94 / 123, hook 250 / 290, difference 157 / 194. Medians sit 28-30 ms under the quiet bar and 43 ms under the refreshing bar. (Architect, plan 2026-11-25.) util steps 1–2 → event → types step 1 → status steps 1–2 → types step 2 → status step 3 → util step 3 (retire `sessionIdleLimit`) → src/observer → page → tools/observer → cli/hook steps 1–2 → cli → cli/hook step 3 (measurement) → install → cli/init → upgrade → src (startServer: end-session tool and the hooks enums). Between plans, consumers of a changed contract in a later plan fail `tsc` until that plan runs; each step runs its own module's tests.

    *Amendment 2026-11-25 (architect, fix loop):* the fix-loop steps run as event step 8 → `npm run build` → cli/hook step 4 (measurement and the #103 numbers); cli step 2 (`view`) and tools/observer step 5 (end-session failure reply) are independent of those and of each other. Then QA re-checks src/cli, src/cli/hook and src/tools/observer, and the speed test is run as #103 says.

    *Amendment 2026-11-25 (architect, second fix loop):* event step 9 → `npm run build` → cli/hook step 5 (guards, reworked speed test, run and record); cli/hook step 4's run box is satisfied by step 5's run.

105. **Per-user root on Windows and the override.** (User, 2026-11-25, packaged-app AppData fix; amends commitment 7.) The Claude desktop app is an MSIX package, so every process it starts (MCP server, spawned observer, hooks) has its writes under `%LOCALAPPDATA%` silently redirected to `%LOCALAPPDATA%\Packages\Claude_<id>\LocalCache\Local\`, while a terminal session writes the real folder; the two never saw each other, so the one-session HALT and the one-observer rule failed across them. `util/runtimePaths/shared/resolveAppDataRoot` now answers, in order: `AIDEMD_HOME` when set, non-empty and absolute, used as the root itself on every OS (empty or relative is ignored); Windows `<homedir()>\.aidemd` (`LOCALAPPDATA` is no longer read); macOS `~/Library/Application Support/aidemd` and Linux `$XDG_STATE_HOME/aidemd` else `~/.local/state/aidemd`, both unchanged. Tests and the release-check harness isolate the root with `AIDEMD_HOME=<temp>/aidemd`, never with `LOCALAPPDATA`/`XDG_STATE_HOME`.

106. **One-time move from the old Windows root.** (User, 2026-11-25: "Move it once"; rules by the architect.) `util/runtimePaths/moveLegacyRoot() → Promise<LegacyMove>` moves what the calling process sees under the pre-fix root (`LOCALAPPDATA` absolute else `<home>\AppData\Local`, joined with `aidemd`; a packaged process sees its redirected copy) into the new root. It runs only on Windows with `AIDEMD_HOME` unset, and does nothing when both roots are the same path. Per project folder: `events/` and `archive/` logs (`.jsonl`) merge as append-only files (target missing: copy; target a byte prefix of the source: replace with the source; same or source a prefix of the target: nothing to copy; anything else: `diverged`, both kept); `presence/` and `ended/` records (`.json`) move unless the new root already holds the file, which then wins. Every copy goes to a `.moving` temp sibling and is published whole (hard link, exclusive; rename only to replace a shorter log). The source is unlinked only after a fresh comparison shows the new root holds all of its bytes; otherwise it stays (`changed`) and is retried on a later start. The old root's `observer.json` and `observer-token` are deleted (user, 2026-11-25: "Delete it too"; the new observer creates its own token, commitment 54); unknown files stay. `shared/runtimeNames` gains `OBSERVER_RECORD_FILE` and `OBSERVER_TOKEN_FILE`, the latter equal to `loadToken`'s file name. Emptied folders are removed with non-recursive `rmdir`. No marker file: a completed move leaves nothing to move, so later calls answer `none`; two processes moving together are safe through the exclusive publish. `LegacyMove = { outcome: "none" } | { outcome: "moved"; legacyRoot; moved; superseded; left: { path; reason: "diverged" | "changed" | "error"; detail? }[] }`.

107. **The move runs at session start, best effort.** (Architect, plan 2026-11-25.) `aide_observer_start` calls `start/moveLegacyHistory` as its first action, before the port resolution, `takeOver` and the HALT check, so the first start after the upgrade judges the merged history. It never blocks or changes the reply (commitment 97 unchanged): files left in place, or a throw, produce one stderr line beginning `aide: `. The hook, `aidemd-mcp view`, the observer process and server boot do not move anything.

108. **Build order for the AppData fix.** (Architect, plan 2026-11-25.) Repo test safety net (Vitest setup gives every test file a temp `AIDEMD_HOME` unless it stubs its own; outside every module spec) → util step 1 → util step 2 → event, src/observer, cli/hook, cli and src test sweeps (independent of each other) → tools/observer → `npm run build` → cli/hook speed test → release-check harness update and `[migration]` CHANGELOG entry (main agent). Until every sweep has run, a Windows test run of an unswept file would resolve the real `%USERPROFILE%\.aidemd` without the safety net, which is why the safety net comes first.

## Cross-module contracts

- `event` exposes record (used by the `aide_observer_event` tool), read-all-sessions with a live flag per session (used by the observer replay and listing), delete-session (used by the observer's one control), the shared event schema, the wire contract of commitment 17, the liveness rule (`shared/judgeLiveness`, commitment 18) and the one reader of `observer.json` (`shared/readObserverRecord`). (Settled 2026-11-22; amended 2026-11-23.) `src/observer` exports `findRunningObserver()`, no project root, reading the root-level `observer.json` plus a `GET /` probe and returning the running observer's address, token and version (or null); its own single-instance check, the version replace of commitment 34 and the `aide_observer_start` tool all use it. `event/read` also returns the project listing of commitment 35.
- `kanban` exposes board-by-column, one task's full note by slug, or `unavailable` with reason; it consumes only the brain path and tag the caller passes.
- `status` consumes project files, the event list and the board result as given, and returns the full view model (intent tree of every AIDE file with its status and progress, the file to surface per commitment 25, plans/todos with progress, session record, sessions with liveness, running work, waiting gates, history).
- `src/observer` consumes all three and the shared event schema; it writes the root-level `observer.json` and nothing else outside the browser stream, and answers the shutdown request of commitment 34.
- `src/observer/page` (browser bundle) consumes the observer's JSON API and stream only, plus these pure modules bundled into it: `event/shared/protocol` (token header), `status/buildHistory/formatDuration`, `status/shared/phaseLabels`, `util/parseFrontmatter`, `util/parseBody`, `install/shared/aidePackage`, `src/observer/shared/pageTitle` (amended 2026-11-23: `buildHistory` itself is no longer imported, `pageTitle` is); server types are imported type-only. Any of those modules gaining a `node:` import breaks the bundle.
- The `/aide` skill (`.claude/skills/aide/SKILL.md`, prose, edited directly by the main agent, not through the pipeline) owns the boot order in commitment 3 and the per-action reports. The observer tools (now two: start and event) need a `CHANGELOG.md` `## [Unreleased]` entry; no `[migration]` entry, since the per-project form never shipped.
- (Observer pass, 2026-11-25.) `event` also exposes `touchPresence` (used by `src/cli/hook`), `endSession` (used by `aide_observer_end_session`), `shared/readPresence`, `shared/readTranscriptActivity` and the rewritten `judgeLiveness` (commitment 76, used by `event/read`, the start tool's HALT check and `src/observer`). `event/read` returns liveness per session instead of a live flag.
- (Observer pass, 2026-11-25.) `status` consumes, in addition, each session's liveness and the project's earlier-session events (commitment 79) as given by `src/observer`; it never imports `event`.
- (Observer pass, 2026-11-25.) `src/observer` adds the presence ping route (commitment 75), hands `status` liveness and every kept session's events, and walks the port list (commitment 73) on start.
- (Observer pass, 2026-11-25.) `src/tools/observer` (new spec) owns `aide_observer_start` (port list, HALT, blocker), `aide_observer_event` (session id in the reply, refusal once ended) and `aide_observer_end_session`; it reaches presence only through `event`. `src/startServer` registers the end-session tool.
- (Observer pass, 2026-11-25.) `src/cli/hook` (new spec) is the `aidemd-mcp hook` entry: reads Claude Code hook JSON on stdin, maps it (commitment 78), writes presence through `event`, links sessions (commitment 71), pings the observer (commitment 75). `src/cli` dispatches to it.
- (Observer pass, 2026-11-25.) `src/service/install` and `src/tools/upgrade` own the `hooks` category (commitment 77) and ship the `aide-observer` skill as the seventh skill. `src/util/runtimePaths` adds presence and ended-mark paths; `src/types` re-exports the new types.
- (Observer pass, 2026-11-25; replaces the `/aide` skill bullet above.) The new `aide-observer` skill (prose, edited directly by the main agent) owns the boot order of commitment 3, the reporting table in /aide's terms (pipeline kinds of commitment 68 included), the HALT and blocker handling, the restart rule of commitment 21 and the per-session "do not observe this". `/aide` becomes a router invoking `aide-observer`, then `aide`; the `aide` skill keeps no observer text. CHANGELOG needs Added/Changed bullets and `[migration]` entries for the `Observer: off` line (commitment 2) and the hooks category (commitment 77).

- (Architect, plan 2026-11-25.) `event` also exposes `readLiveness` (commitment 96), `shared/readEndedMark`, `shared/formatSessionTag` and `shared/parseSessionTag` (commitment 90), and the schemas `shared/presenceRecord`, `shared/endedMark`, `shared/presencePing`. `ProjectLog` gains `unlinked: UnlinkedPresence[]`; `SessionLog.live` becomes `liveness: SessionLiveness`.
- (Architect, plan 2026-11-25.) `status` exposes `shared/findCurrentPipeline` and `shared/findOpenPhase` (commitment 100), used by `src/tools/observer/start` for the HALT's pipeline and phase.
- (Architect, plan 2026-11-25.) `src/service/install/shared/planHooksMerge` (commitment 102) is consumed by `src/tools/upgrade`'s hooks category.
- (Architect, plan 2026-11-25.) `src/startServer` (helper of the `src` module, planned in `src/plan.aide`) registers `aide_observer_end_session`; its description and the reworded start and event descriptions are prose for the main agent.
- (Architect, AppData fix 2026-11-25.) `src/util/runtimePaths` exposes `moveLegacyRoot` (commitment 106), consumed only by `src/tools/observer/start`; `listProjectFolders` gains an optional `{ root }`; `shared/runtimeNames` gains `OBSERVER_RECORD_FILE` and `OBSERVER_TOKEN_FILE`. The release-check harness (`.claude/skills/release-check/run.mjs`, repo tooling) and `scripts/seedObserver` isolate with `AIDEMD_HOME`.

